# Apple Design Process

Source: https://developer.apple.com/videos/play/wwdc2025/359/

Work the phases in order; each builds on the previous. Never start with visual design.

## Diagnostic — run this before changing anything

A screen must answer all three questions instantly, without the user piecing them together:

1. Where am I?
2. What can I do?
3. Where can I go from here?

A screen can look good and still fail all three. Failure shows up as hesitation and confusion.

## 1. Structure (information architecture)

- Write down everything the product does — features, workflows, nice-to-haves. Don't judge or cut yet.
- Imagine someone else using it: when and where, how it fits their routine, what genuinely helps and what gets in the way.
- Only then clean up: remove what isn't essential, rename what isn't clear, group what naturally belongs together.

If it isn't clear what's essential, it can't be communicated in the interface.

## 2. Navigation

- Every extra navigation entry is one more decision, and can present the product as more complex than it is.
- Navigation is for navigating, **not for actions**. Put a primary action where people will actually use it.
- Use explicit labels and familiar icons. Nobody should have to interact with something to learn what it does.
- Give each screen a title stating where the user is — not branding, not a menu.

## 3. Content

- Never mix distinct content types in one list; split them into sections.
- **Progressive disclosure**: show only what's needed to get started, reveal the rest on interaction.
- Choose layout by function, not appearance. Lists are scannable and compact; grids and collections suit image sets.
- Group content to cut decision overload: by time, by progress, or by pattern/relationship.

## 4. Visual design (last)

- **Squint test**: if your eye jumps to one element and misses the rest, hierarchy is missing.
- Create a visual anchor — make the most important element larger or higher in contrast.
- Prefer system text styles over hand-picked sizes, and honor user text-size preferences.
- Text over imagery needs a subtle gradient or blur behind it for legibility.
- Use semantic colors named by purpose (`label`, `secondarySystemBackground`), not literal colors, so they adapt to contrast and light/dark settings.

Iterate. Not getting it right on the first pass is part of the process.
